export const GUIDE_HTML = "\n<p>A screenshot-illustrated walkthrough: installing lizaIP, setting up your remote's\npages and buttons, the image schemes you can use for icons, and live entity values on\nthe remote face. For the condensed reference (settings table, troubleshooting,\nsupport), see <a href=\"https://github.com/liza-home/home-assistant/blob/main/README.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><code>README.md</code></a>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"getting-started\">Getting started</h2>\n<h3 id=\"install\">Install</h3>\n<p>Requires Home Assistant 2026.11.0+. The\n<a href=\"https://github.com/liza-home/home-assistant/blob/main/README.md#installation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Installation section of <code>README.md</code></a>\nhas a button that opens the Marketplace with this repository filled in. Installing\nneeds no restart; updating a version Home Assistant already runs does, and it asks\nfor one with a repair notice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"provisioning-a-new-device\">Provisioning a new device</h3>\n<p>A brand-new remote is not on your network yet, so there is nothing for Home Assistant\nto discover. The remote opens its own Wi-Fi network instead, and you hand it your home\nnetwork from a page it serves itself. The panel links to a step-by-step guide for\niPhone and Android while no device has been added; the same three steps are below.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Find the lizaIP Wi-Fi.</strong> Open the Wi-Fi settings on your phone or computer and\n   look for an open network named <code>lizaIP-xxxxxx</code>, where the suffix is the remote's\n   own.</li>\n<li><strong>Connect to it.</strong> The network is open, so no Wi-Fi password is needed for this\n   step. Your phone may warn that the network has no internet access; that is expected.</li>\n<li><strong>Open the setup page.</strong> If it isn't shown automatically, go to <code>172.16.10.1</code> in\n   a browser. Then pick your home Wi-Fi, enter its password and confirm the IP\n   settings.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The remote then leaves its own network and joins yours, at which point it announces\nitself and the next section takes over.</p>\n<p>The per-platform walkthrough — iPhone, Android, anything else — lives at\n<a href=\"https://lizaip.api.ruwido.com/setup/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">lizaip.api.ruwido.com/setup</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"scanning-adding-devices\">Scanning / adding devices</h3>\n<p>Once the integration is installed, a <strong>lizaIP</strong> device on your network announces\nitself and Home Assistant discovers it automatically — no manual IP entry needed.</p>\n<p>You do not have to leave the panel to add it, and you do not have to go looking\neither. When a remote turns up on the network, a row for it appears under\n<strong>Discovered</strong> at the top of the panel's device list, above the remotes you have\nalready set up. The row carries <strong>Add</strong>, which sets it up in one click. There is\nnothing to start, and nothing is there when nothing has been found.</p>\n<p>If you have ignored a remote from <strong>Settings → Devices &amp; Services</strong>, it stops being\noffered here too. To bring it back, switch on <strong>Show ignored integrations</strong> in the\nfilter menu beside the search box and select <strong>Stop ignoring</strong>.</p>\n<p>If you are inside a remote when one turns up, the back arrow appears in the toolbar\nto take you to the list — including when that remote is your only one, which the\npanel otherwise opens straight onto.</p>\n<p>A discovered device also still shows up as a card under <strong>Settings → Devices &amp;\nServices</strong>, the same place any other auto-discovered integration appears; confirming\nit there does exactly the same thing.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Settings → Devices &amp; Services, where a discovered lizaIP turns up\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/scanning-devices.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"devices-and-services\">Devices and services</h3>\n<p>The device's own page (<strong>Settings → Devices &amp; Services → lizaIP → <em>device name</em></strong>) is\nwhere you see everything Home Assistant knows about the remote: its entities, firmware\nversion, IP address, and recent activity.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The integration's device list\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/integration-detail.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p><img alt=\"A device's detail page — sensors, controls, diagnostics\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/device-page.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>It lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Controls / Sensors</strong> — one entity per button (for state-based automations), battery\n  level, Wi-Fi strength, and similar diagnostics.</li>\n<li><strong>Configuration</strong> — brightness and automatic-brightness entities, firmware version.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic</strong> — the device's IP address and connection state.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"config\">Config</h3>\n<p>Nothing needs to be configured for the remote to work, but <strong>Settings → Devices &amp;\nServices → lizaIP → Configure</strong> has a few options worth knowing about: device language,\noptimistic updates, the slider update interval, and demo mode. See the\n<a href=\"https://github.com/liza-home/home-assistant/blob/main/README.md#settings\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Settings table in <code>README.md</code></a> for what each one\ndoes.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The lizaIP Options / Configure dialog\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/configure-dialog.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"what-can-you-do\">What can you do</h2>\n<p>Everything below happens in the <strong>lizaIP</strong> panel in the Home Assistant sidebar, which\nopens the editor with its four tabs: <strong>🎛️ Buttons</strong>, <strong>⚡ Actions</strong>, <strong>🐞 Debug</strong> and\nthe gear.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The Buttons tab — remote face and pages\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/panel-overview.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>The tabs sit at the right of the toolbar, above the remote face, next to the gear,\nthe <strong>?</strong> that opens this guide, and the <strong>⋮</strong> menu:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The toolbar's tabs, gear, guide and ⋮ menu\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/toolbar-tabs.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>The <strong>?</strong> opens this guide beside the editor, so you can read it while you work. It\nsearches, remembers where you were, and its pictures open full size when clicked:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The guide, open beside the editor\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/guide-in-panel.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>A page holds <strong>12 freely assignable buttons</strong> plus fixed controls (power, back, voice,\nvolume, and a vertical slider). The strip beside the remote face shows every page —\nclick a thumbnail to edit it, drag to reorder.</p>\n<h3 id=\"linking-to-a-remote-a-page-or-a-button\">Linking to a remote, a page or a button</h3>\n<p>The address bar follows what you have open, so any of it can be bookmarked or\npasted to someone else:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What you are looking at</th>\n<th>URL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The device list</td>\n<td><code>/lizaip</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One remote</td>\n<td><code>/lizaip/&lt;id&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A tab of it</td>\n<td><code>/lizaip/&lt;id&gt;/actions</code>, <code>…/settings</code>, <code>…/debug</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A page</td>\n<td><code>/lizaip/&lt;id&gt;/buttons/&lt;page_id&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A button on that page</td>\n<td><code>/lizaip/&lt;id&gt;/buttons/&lt;page_id&gt;/button_3</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>&lt;id&gt;</code> is the remote's own short id — the hex in its name, for example\n<code>1c63499a7193</code> — so you can tell from a link which remote it opens. The long\nconfig-entry id works too, which is what links written by older versions carry,\nand capitalisation does not matter either way.</p>\n<p>If a link names a remote, page or button that is no longer there, the panel\nopens as much of it as still exists rather than failing: an unknown page falls\nback to the remote's first page, and an unknown remote to whichever remote you\nalready had open, or the one you last worked on, or the only one you have. You\nland on the device list only when there is genuinely nothing to prefer.</p>\n<p>Leaving the panel for another part of Home Assistant and coming back returns\nyou where you were, including the page and button. Clicking <strong>lizaIP</strong> in the\nsidebar while the panel is already open goes back to the device list instead —\nthat click is how you get out of a remote without hunting for the back arrow.\nIf you only have one remote paired there is no list to show, so that click\ntakes you to its pages, cleared of whatever you had selected.</p>\n<h3 id=\"define-pages\">Define pages</h3>\n<p>A remote with no pages configured does not sit blank. It shows a stand-in page\ninstead, headed by the Home Assistant logo and <strong>welcome!</strong> in your language, with\nthe remote's own IP address, its firmware version, the address of your Home Assistant\nand its battery on its buttons. It is a placeholder, not a page — the panel still\nshows an empty page strip with nothing but the <strong>+</strong> tile, and there is nothing there\nto edit or delete. Add your first page and it is gone; delete your last one and it\ncomes back. Its buttons do nothing, as they only show values.</p>\n<p>Click the <strong>+</strong> tile at the end of the page strip to add a page: start <strong>blank</strong>, or\npick a <strong>template</strong> — a ready-made page for a device you already have in Home Assistant\n(for example, a Sonos speaker with its favourites already laid out). A Sonos page is\ntitled with the Sonos logo and the speaker's name, and its Previous, Play/Pause and\nNext buttons show the previous, current and next track as their tooltips (see\n<a href=\"#track-names-current-previous-next\">Track names</a>).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A page <strong>template</strong> here is a ready-made page. It has nothing to do with Home\nAssistant's <strong>template helpers</strong> (the Jinja sensors further down this guide), which\nshare the word.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><img alt=\"The Add page dialog — blank page or template\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/add-page-dialog.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>The templates on offer, and the Home Assistant integration each one needs — a template\nonly lists devices of its integration. Templates for the same device family share one\ncard: the Sonos templates are behind a single <strong>Sonos</strong> card, which opens them, with\nthe three-page <strong>Sonos</strong> first.\nThose cards come from folders under <code>assets/templates/</code>, and they nest as\ndeeply as the folders do.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Template</th>\n<th>For</th>\n<th>What the page holds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Table of contents</strong></td>\n<td>— (needs no device)</td>\n<td>A button for each of this remote's pages, on buttons 1–12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sonos</strong></td>\n<td>Sonos</td>\n<td>Three pages: the album art on buttons 1, 2, 5 and 6, Playlists and Radio on 3 and 4, Previous, Play/Pause, Next and Mute on 9–12; behind it a Playlists and a Radio page, eleven favourites to a page with button 12 leading to the next (twelve on the last) (more pages come and go with your favourites, up to three); Back goes back a page, and from the first to Now Playing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sonos Control</strong></td>\n<td>Sonos</td>\n<td>One page: your favourites on buttons 1–8; Previous, Play/Pause, Next and Mute on 9–12; volume</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sonos Radio</strong></td>\n<td>Sonos</td>\n<td>Only your favourite radio stations, on buttons 1–12; volume. More stations get more pages, swiped to (up to three)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sonos Playlists</strong></td>\n<td>Sonos</td>\n<td>Only your favourite playlists, on buttons 1–12; volume. More playlists get more pages, swiped to (up to three)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sonos Albums</strong>, <strong>Tracks</strong>, <strong>Podcasts</strong>, <strong>Audiobooks</strong></td>\n<td>Sonos</td>\n<td>Only your favourites of that kind, on buttons 1–12; volume. More get more pages, swiped to (up to three). Each is offered only for a speaker that has favourites of its kind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sonos Cover</strong></td>\n<td>Sonos</td>\n<td>The album art of what is playing across buttons 1–3, 5–7 and 9–11; Previous, Play/Pause and Next on 4, 8 and 12; volume</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sonos Options</strong></td>\n<td>Sonos</td>\n<td>Mute, shuffle, repeat, volume presets and a sleep timer for one speaker (<strong>Add options…</strong> only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Philips Hue</strong></td>\n<td>Hue</td>\n<td>Every lamp on a bridge, one per button; a bridge with more than twelve gets more pages, swiped to (up to three)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hue Lamp Options</strong></td>\n<td>Hue</td>\n<td>Brightness and colour-temperature presets for one lamp (<strong>Add options…</strong> only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Android TV</strong></td>\n<td>Android TV Remote</td>\n<td>Laid out as the LG webOS TV template: remote keys and volume, with an <strong>Apps</strong> subpage (the apps configured in the integration's options) on button 4 and a <strong>Digits</strong> keypad with Channel up and down on button 8. The integration lists no inputs, so button 12 opens the TV's own input menu. Back returns to the remote keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Apple TV</strong></td>\n<td>Apple TV</td>\n<td>Laid out as the LG webOS TV template: remote keys, volume and play/pause, with an <strong>Apps</strong> subpage (its apps, with their App Store icons — App Store and Arcade show their own logos; Computers, Settings and Search, which the store does not list, get a lookalike in their home-screen colours) on button 4. An Apple TV has no digit keys and no inputs. Back returns to the remote keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Amazon Fire TV</strong></td>\n<td>Android Debug Bridge</td>\n<td>Laid out as the LG webOS TV template: remote keys, volume and play/pause, with an <strong>Apps</strong> subpage (the apps it lists as sources) on button 4 and a <strong>Digits</strong> keypad with Channel up and down on button 8. Back returns to the remote keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Roku</strong></td>\n<td>Roku</td>\n<td>Laid out as the LG webOS TV template: remote keys, volume and play/pause, with an <strong>Apps</strong> subpage (its apps and channels) on button 4 and, for a Roku TV, an <strong>Inputs</strong> subpage (Antenna TV, HDMI 1–4, AV) on button 12. A Roku has no digit keys. Back returns to the remote keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Samsung TV</strong></td>\n<td>Samsung Smart TV</td>\n<td>Laid out as the LG webOS TV template: remote keys and volume, with an <strong>Apps</strong> subpage on button 4 (its apps, with the icons from Samsung's TV app store — a newer Samsung TV does not list its apps, so it is asked about every app of the store; the first look takes about half a minute, and an app built into the TV, such as Samsung TV Plus, does not show), an <strong>Inputs</strong> subpage (TV and HDMI) on button 12 and a <strong>Digits</strong> keypad with Channel up and down on button 8. Back returns to the remote keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sony Bravia TV</strong></td>\n<td>Sony Bravia TV</td>\n<td>Laid out as the LG webOS TV template: the TV's remote keys and volume, with an <strong>Apps</strong> subpage (its apps) on button 4, an <strong>Inputs</strong> subpage (its inputs, as named on the TV) on button 12 and a <strong>Digits</strong> keypad with Channel up and down on button 8. Back returns to the remote keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hisense TV (Vidaa)</strong></td>\n<td>Hisense TV (<a href=\"https://github.com/sehaas/ha_hisense_tv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HACS</a>)</td>\n<td>Laid out as the LG webOS TV template: the TV's remote keys and volume, with an <strong>Apps</strong> subpage (the apps in the integration's media browser) on button 4, an <strong>Inputs</strong> subpage (its sources) on button 12 and a <strong>Digits</strong> keypad with Channel up and down on button 8. The keys are MQTT messages on the integration's default prefix <code>hisense</code>; a TV set up with another prefix needs it changed in a copy of the template</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>LG webOS TV</strong></td>\n<td>LG webOS TV</td>\n<td>The TV's remote keys and volume, Power toggling it (turning it on needs a Wake-on-LAN automation); then subpages reached from the remote: <strong>Apps</strong> (button 4) with the TV's installed apps alphabetically on buttons 1–11 (and a twelfth on button 12) — a TV with more gets another Apps page for every eleven more (up to three), button 12 leading on to the next and Back to the one before; the pages come and go as apps are installed and removed — <strong>Inputs</strong> (button 12) with the TV's HDMI and other inputs, and a <strong>Digits</strong> keypad (button 8) with Channel up and down. The TV's own tools (app store, browser, camera, Multi View, Teletext, …) are left off the Apps pages; the list is the template's <code>exclude:</code>. Back returns to the page you came from</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Every TV's <strong>Apps</strong> page holds eleven apps, and the last one a twelfth on button 12; a TV with more gets more Apps pages (up to\nthree), button 12 leading on to the next and Back going back a page, and from the first\nto the remote keys. Pages like these come and go as their items do: they are counted\nwhenever the remote refreshes, and while the device cannot be reached, nothing changes. A TV\nthat is off keeps the apps, and their icons, it showed last. Where a TV lists Android's own\nlauncher, store and settings, or its maker's demo and help tiles, among its apps, those are\nleft off the page, so the apps you installed are not crowded out.</p>\n<p>A template is only offered while Home Assistant has a device it can be bound to — without\na Sonos speaker, the Sonos templates are simply not in the list.</p>\n<p>A template may bring <strong>several pages at once</strong> — the dialog shows how many after its\nname, as in <strong>Sonos (3 pages)</strong>: the page you land on plus subpages, already linked, for the same device. They belong together — <strong>Change device…</strong> on the\npage you land on moves the whole set, and deleting it deletes its subpages, which nothing\nelse would lead to, and the dialog says so before you confirm. Each page is ordinary otherwise: change any\nbutton, or delete a subpage on its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Table of contents</strong> lists the remote's pages: each of buttons 1–12 goes to one of\nits pages, in the order the pages have, and shows the picture from that page's title (its\nlogo or icon) — or, for a title of words alone, a short text of the page's name. Subpages\nare not listed. It keeps up by itself: add, remove or rename a page and the table of\ncontents follows; moving pages around does not reorder it until the next page is added,\nremoved or renamed. Delete one of its buttons and it stays empty — the table of contents\nleaves that button alone from then on.</p>\n<p><strong>Showcase</strong> sits beside Template in the same dialog: pages that show off what a tile can\ndo with <a href=\"#how-to-use-variables-with-examples\">variables</a> and\n<a href=\"#how-to-use-mdi-text-media\">pictures</a>. Add one, then open any button to see how it\nis written, and copy what you like.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Showcase</th>\n<th>For</th>\n<th>What it shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>This remote</strong></td>\n<td>nothing — added straight away</td>\n<td>The remote's own battery as a value, a fill level, an icon picked by arithmetic and a history graph; whether it is charging or in a hand; its address and firmware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Weather</strong></td>\n<td>nothing — added straight away</td>\n<td>Home Assistant's weather as its icon, in words, in °C and °F; humidity and cloud cover as fill levels; wind, pressure — no template helpers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Picture lab</strong></td>\n<td>nothing — added straight away</td>\n<td>Logos, tints, backgrounds, the state colours, fill levels and combined pictures side by side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Light studio</strong></td>\n<td>a lamp from Hue, HomeKit or Nanoleaf</td>\n<td>Live brightness and colour temperature, brightness presets drawn as fill levels, tinted colour-temperature presets, the lamp's model and firmware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Media wall</strong></td>\n<td>Sonos</td>\n<td>The cover over a 2×2 block, volume and track progress as fill levels, the artist as words, previous and next track names</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A showcase that needs no device looks up what it shows when you add it — this remote's\nown sensors, the first weather service — and writes those into its tiles; something your\nhome does not have leaves its tile empty.\nAfter that the tiles are ordinary values — point one at another sensor in its Icon field.</p>\n<p>The favourites on the Sonos pages stay live: rename or reorder one in the Sonos app and\nthe buttons follow. On <strong>Sonos Cover</strong> the nine tiles are a picture, not buttons —\npressing one does nothing, and the picture changes with the track (see\n<a href=\"#spreading-one-picture-over-several-buttons\">Spreading one picture over several buttons</a>\nfor how it is cut). Every template page is an ordinary page once added: change any button\nas you like.</p>\n<p>Right-click (or long-press) a page in the strip for its menu: <strong>Make subpage</strong> (or\n<strong>Make main page</strong>), <strong>Assign device…</strong> for a page that has no device yet — or\n<strong>Change device…</strong> for one that has — then <strong>Duplicate</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Assign device…</strong> gives a page you built yourself a device, as a template page has\none. The dialog offers every device and proposes the one most of the page's buttons\nalready control. From then on <code>${_device}</code> and <code>${_device.…}</code> in the page's title and\nbuttons read that device, the page card names it, and <strong>Change device…</strong> moves the\npage to another device: buttons aimed at the old device's entities are pointed at the\nnew device's, everything else stays as it is.</p>\n<p>With no button selected, the card for that page shows <strong>Page settings</strong> — its\n<strong>title</strong>, and the page's device if it has one. Click the title to open its own editor: plain text, an\n<code>mdi:</code> icon, a <code>logo:</code> name, or an image URL (see\n<a href=\"#how-to-use-mdi-text-media\">How to use mdi, text, media</a> below).</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Editing a page title — here set to logo:sonos ${_device}\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/page-title-editor.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"assigning-actions-to-buttons\">Assigning actions to buttons</h3>\n<p>Click any button on the remote face to select it. The card below shows that button's\n<strong>Icon</strong> and <strong>Tooltip</strong> fields, and the action it performs.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"A button selected, with its image, tooltip and action\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/assign-action.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>Before you click anything, you can also just <strong>point at a button</strong> — rest the mouse on\na shape on the open page (or tab to it with the keyboard) and a small card appears\nbeside it summarising that button: its tooltip and icon, every press it runs (a toggle\ncycle is listed in the order it cycles — the first four, then a count of the rest),\nits long touch, what entity it acts on, and\nwhich page it opens. A button that opens a page that no longer exists says so, which\nis worth knowing because the remote ignores such a press. A button with nothing on it\nsimply says <em>No action assigned</em>. Move away or press <kbd>Esc</kbd> to dismiss it.</p>\n<p>Sliders get the same card, worded for what they are: it says whether the slider\nfollows the selected button or is fixed to one device, and names that device and\nthe property it moves.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Icon</strong> — what the button shows: an <code>mdi:</code> icon, a <code>logo:</code>, static <code>text:</code>, or a\n  live <code>media:</code> thumbnail. See <a href=\"#how-to-use-mdi-text-media\">the image schemes section</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tooltip</strong> — what is shown on long-press / hover, and what screen readers announce.\n  Usually words, but it takes every image scheme the Icon field does (an <code>mdi:</code>\n  icon, a <code>logo:</code>, …).\n  It can reference a live entity value, as this screenshot's hint line shows\n  (<code>Now reads **goto page: subpage**</code>) — see\n  <a href=\"#how-to-use-variables-with-examples\">How to use variables</a>.\n  A tooltip is shown as it is written, with nothing put in front of it: if it is\n  only placeholders and they read as nothing right now, the tooltip is empty. Left\n  empty, a button's tooltip is worked out for you from what it does (\"Mute\", \"Off\",\n  \"CH+\"); a button with nothing to say stays empty.</li>\n<li>Expand the action row to pick what the button does — call any Home Assistant\n  service, or a built-in action such as <strong>goto page</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Key repeat</strong> — holding the button runs its action again and again, like\n  the arrow keys of a TV remote: after half a second, 30 times a second until you let\n  go; a short tap still runs it once. Both numbers are set per remote in the gear tab,\n  under <strong>Buttons</strong> (initial delay 100–2000 ms, rate 1–50 per second). Each press waits\n  for the one before it, so a device that answers slowly repeats more slowly rather\n  than falling behind. The TV templates switch it on for their arrow and volume keys.\n  A button with a long touch does not repeat: holding it is its long touch.</li>\n<li><strong>Add options…</strong> — right-click (or long-press) a button on the face to give it an\n  options page: a subpage right after the current page, which the button's\n  <strong>long touch</strong> opens. Only the options its own device answers are offered —\n  <strong>Hue Lamp Options</strong> for a Hue lamp, <strong>Sonos Options</strong> for a speaker — and the\n  page is bound to that device without asking. When only one fits, it is added\n  straight away, with no dialog. Those options templates are\n  offered here only; they are not pages to swipe to, so the <strong>Add page</strong> button\n  leaves them out. The new page's hardware <strong>Back</strong> button leads back to the\n  page the button sits on, whatever the template itself puts there. Offered while\n  <strong>Long touch</strong> and <strong>Options pages</strong> are both switched on for the remote (gear\n  tab → Buttons), the button has no long touch yet, and an options template fits\n  its device. Delete the options page and the long touch goes with it, so the\n  button can be given a new one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every action you assign is also listed in the <strong>Actions</strong> tab, the library of actions\nin use across all buttons and pages:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The Action Library tab\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/action-library.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>Open a row to rename it (the name tooltips are derived from), set its default icons, or\nsee every button it's assigned to. This is where you curate actions, not where you\ncreate new ones — new actions are created by assigning them to a button first.</p>\n<p>The vertical slider has its own card when selected: pick the entity it drives, what it\ncontrols (brightness, volume, cover position — options depend on what the entity\nsupports), and a sensitivity.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The vertical slider's own configuration\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/slider-config.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"let-an-ai-assistant-build-pages\">Let an AI assistant build pages</h3>\n<p>lizaIP offers its own tools to the AI assistants in Home Assistant, so you can ask for a\npage in your own words instead of building it button by button:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>\"Make me a page to control my shades.\"</em></li>\n<li><em>\"Create a page which lets me control my Sonos in the Wohnzimmer.\"</em></li>\n<li><em>\"Put the bedroom lamp on button 5 of the Licht page.\"</em></li>\n<li><em>\"Call button 3 'Kino' and give it the Netflix logo.\"</em> — only the tooltip and the\n  picture change; the button still does what it did.</li>\n<li><em>\"Swap buttons 1 and 4\"</em>, <em>\"move the Sonos page to the front\"</em>, <em>\"rename the Licht page\n  to Lampen\"</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Setting it up.</strong> In Home Assistant, open <strong>Settings → Voice assistants</strong>, open the\nconversation agent you use (Anthropic Claude, OpenAI, Google, Ollama …), and under\n<strong>Control Home Assistant</strong> tick <strong>liza</strong> beside <strong>Assist</strong>. To use Claude Desktop or\nClaude Code instead, add Home Assistant's <strong>Model Context Protocol Server</strong> integration and\nchoose <strong>liza</strong> as its API there.</p>\n<p><strong>What the assistant does.</strong> For a speaker, a TV or Hue lights\nit uses the matching <a href=\"#define-pages\">template</a>, bound to the device you named — \"in the\nWohnzimmer\" picks the speaker in that room. Otherwise it builds an ordinary page button by\nbutton: each button gets an action, the entity it acts on, a short tooltip and an icon, and a\nshade or lamp button can steer the vertical slider while it is selected. Either way the page\nis added at the end of the remote's pages, as if you had made it yourself, and you can edit\nit in the panel like any other.</p>\n<p><strong>What it may not do.</strong> It only sees and uses the entities you exposed to that assistant\n(<strong>Settings → Voice assistants → Expose</strong>). Changing a remote needs an administrator account\nbehind the request. It never changes a page you did not name, and it deletes a page only\nwhen it names both the page's number and its title.</p>\n<h3 id=\"config-gear-tab\">Config (gear tab)</h3>\n<p>The gear tab picks the remote's <strong>theme</strong> — which is where the page title and tooltip\nfont and size live — and a couple of button-wide behaviours, such as whether buttons\nsupport <strong>long touch</strong> (a second action on press-and-hold).</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The Settings / gear tab — theme and long touch\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/settings-tab.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"themes\">Themes</h3>\n<p>A <strong>theme</strong> is one named set of the defaults the remote draws with: the colour\nswatches you pick a tint from, the colours an on, off or unavailable button is\ndrawn in (and the default for one whose state says nothing), the font and size of titles and tooltips, and the default\non/off icons. You pick one per remote at the top of the gear tab's\n<strong>Appearance</strong> page.</p>\n<p>A theme only replaces what the integration would otherwise decide on its own.\nA page's default colour, a button's own picture, colour or font, and the live\ncolour a lamp's brightness gives its icon all keep winning, so switching a\ntheme never overwrites a choice you made about a button or a page.</p>\n<p><strong>Default</strong> is built in and is what a remote that has never picked one uses.\nTwo more presets ship with the integration, and each changes something you\ncan see before you have coloured a single button: <strong>Monochrome</strong> draws\neverything in grey and <strong>High Contrast</strong> uses bolder colours and larger text.</p>\n<p>A palette on its own is invisible on a remote whose buttons you have not\ncoloured by hand, because an ordinary tile is drawn in <code>on</code>. A theme\nmeant to look different therefore sets that, not only its swatches.</p>\n<h4 id=\"picking-one\">Picking one</h4>\n<p>The card offers one row of themes. Each is shown as what it draws — its\ncolours, its title text at its own size, and its own on/off glyphs — so the\nchoice is made by looking rather than by reading a name. Click one and the\nremote is given it at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong> underneath shows the whole remote under the theme you are trying:\nthe page title, a row of buttons in each colour, and the tooltip. Every\npicture in it is drawn by the integration for this remote, so it is what the\ndevice will show rather than an impression of it.</p>\n<p>The pick is the whole gesture: there is no <strong>Apply</strong> to press afterwards and\nno <strong>Cancel</strong>. Clicking the theme the remote is already on writes nothing, and\npicking the one you came from puts it back.</p>\n<p>A shipped theme is never changed. Each one carries only an eye and a copy\nicon: the eye opens it read-only, the copy opens the custom editor with that\ntheme already loaded, so your changes become a theme of your own and the\noriginal stays as it was. A theme of your own carries a copy icon, a pencil,\na download icon and a bin. The download saves the theme as <code>&lt;id&gt;.yaml</code>, the\nsame file it is kept as.</p>\n<p>To add a theme file you were given, drag it onto the dashed <strong>Create…</strong>\ntile at the end of the row: it lights up while the file is over it, and the\ntheme is added as one of your own. It never replaces a theme already there —\na name in use is taken up a number (<em>Monochrome 2</em>) — and a file that is not\na theme is refused with the reason. Clicking the tile still opens the editor\non a new theme.</p>\n<p>The title and tooltip are the theme's, so there are no per-remote <strong>Page\ntitle</strong> and <strong>Tooltip</strong> cards — there would be nothing to decide. Their fonts\nand sizes are set in the theme's own <strong>Text size</strong> section.</p>\n<h4 id=\"reading-a-theme-that-came-with-the-integration\">Reading a theme that came with the integration</h4>\n<p>A preset shows three of its colours and a line of its text on the tile, which\nis enough to pick one but not enough to copy it. Clicking the <strong>eye</strong> on a\npreset opens it in the editor headed <strong>View theme — <name></strong>, with every value\nfilled in and nothing editable: the palette with its hex codes, the three\nstate colours, both fonts and sizes, and the on/off icons. The sections still\nopen and close, so you can read the part you came for.</p>\n<p>Nothing there can be saved, because the file ships with the integration and an\nupdate would write over it. <strong>Make a copy I can change</strong> at the bottom hands\nstraight to the editor on a new theme of your own, started from what you were\nreading.</p>\n<h4 id=\"building-one-of-your-own\">Building one of your own</h4>\n<p><strong>Create…</strong> at the end of the row opens the editor on a new theme, headed\n<strong>New theme</strong> and ending in <strong>Create theme</strong>. It always starts a new file,\nwhatever the remote is wearing at the time. Make as many as you like. Pointing\nat one of yours and clicking the pencil opens the same editor headed\n<strong>Edit theme — <name></strong>, ending in <strong>Save</strong>, so the two are never confused.</p>\n<p><strong>Start from</strong> picks the theme the draft begins as — any of the presets, or\n<strong>Blank</strong> to start under no other theme's name. Either way the draft opens on\na full set of colours, sizes and icons, so every field is there to change.\nThen name it: the name has to be free, and the editor says so under the field\nwhile it is not.</p>\n<p>Underneath are three sections, one per part: <strong>Colours</strong>, <strong>Text size</strong> and\n<strong>Icons</strong>. Each opens to the fields that set that part by hand.\nSetting one part leaves the other two alone, so a theme can take the colours\nit started from and still have sizes of its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Colours</strong> sets two different things, and names them apart.</p>\n<p><em>Tile colour by state</em> is the tint a button takes by itself from what the\nentity behind it is doing — <strong>On</strong>, <strong>Off</strong>, <strong>Unavailable</strong>, and <strong>Anything\nelse</strong> for a button with no entity at all.</p>\n<p>These are what a button <em>falls back to</em>, not the last word. A theme sits below\nevery choice already made:</p>\n<pre><code>built-in defaults → theme → settings → a page's icon colour\n  → the button's own icon colour → the live entity\n</code></pre>\n<p>So a page given an icon colour draws in that colour rather than in the state\ncolour; an icon written with a colour of its own (<code>mdi:lightbulb?fg=blue</code>)\nkeeps it; and with <strong>Tint icons with the light's colour</strong> switched on, a lit\nlamp's own colour wins over all of them. Picking a theme never overrides\nsomething you set by hand — it only replaces what would otherwise be a\nconstant.</p>\n<p><em>Colours a page can be given</em> are the six swatches offered under a page's icon\ncolour. The caption above each says what colour it now holds, so a swatch\npainted red reads <strong>Red</strong>. Behind them the integration keeps six fixed keys —\n<code>white</code>, <code>yellow</code>, <code>blue</code>, <code>green</code>, <code>red</code>, <code>inactive</code> — which is what a\nhand-written icon asks for, as <code>mdi:lightbulb?fg=white</code> does. A theme repaints\na key but never renames it. A page stores the colour it picked, not the key,\nso repainting a swatch here leaves pages already set alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Icons</strong> follows the same rule: the glyph is the one a button falls\nback to when it names none of its own. An icon set on\nthe button wins, and an empty field keeps the built-in glyph.</p>\n<p>Rather than list every icon the integration knows, the section asks <strong>which\naction</strong> you want to change the icons for, and then shows that action's own\nicons only. A lamp offers <strong>On</strong> and <strong>Off</strong>; a mute button offers muted and\nunmuted; an action that has no states of its own offers the one icon its\ndomain falls back to. The action is only a way of finding the icon — what the\ntheme stores is the icon itself, so a theme stays portable between remotes\nthat have entirely different actions.</p>\n<p>Because you are looking through one action at a time, everything the theme\nchanges is listed under the fields as well, each with a cross to put it back.\nThat list is the record of what the theme does to icons, whichever action you\nhappen to be looking through.</p>\n<p><strong>Save</strong> writes it to <code>config/lizaip/themes/</code> as a file you can also edit by\nhand, and applies it to the remote straight away, so <strong>Result</strong> shows what you\njust built. Every remote already drawing with that theme is redrawn with the\nedit. A theme of your own can be deleted from the editor or with its bin; the\nremotes that were using it fall back to the default look at once.</p>\n<p>A remote set up before the picker was simplified may still hold a mix of three\nthemes. It keeps drawing exactly as it did, and is shown as <strong>Your earlier\nmix</strong> so the card tells the truth; its copy icon lifts the mix into a theme of\nyour own. Picking any theme replaces it.</p>\n<h4 id=\"writing-one-by-hand\">Writing one by hand</h4>\n<p>Put a <code>.yaml</code> file in <code>config/lizaip/themes/</code> and restart Home Assistant. The\nfile name, without the extension, is the theme's id; <code>name:</code> is what the picker\nshows. Every section is optional — leave one out and that part stays as it is.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">name: My Theme\n\ncolors:\n  # The swatches the colour picker offers, in this order. A name of your own\n  # invention is re-pointed everywhere, so a button set to `amber` follows the\n  # theme; the built-in hues keep their colour so old pages do not change.\n  palette:\n    - { name: white, hex: &quot;FFFFFF&quot; }\n    - { name: amber, hex: &quot;FFB347&quot; }\n  # How an on, off or unavailable button is drawn, and `default` for one whose\n  # state says nothing (no entity, or an action such as Next track). Quote\n  # &quot;on&quot; and &quot;off&quot;: YAML reads them bare as true and false.\n  states:\n    &quot;on&quot;: &quot;FFFFFF&quot;\n    &quot;off&quot;: &quot;8C8FA6&quot;\n    unavailable: &quot;964A4C&quot;\n    default: &quot;FFFFFF&quot;\n\ntext:\n  title:   { font: roboto-light, font_size: 48 }\n  tooltip: { font: roboto-light, font_size: 24 }\n\nicons:\n  # Only the rows you want changed; everything else keeps its usual icon.\n  domain_state_icons:\n    light:\n      &quot;on&quot;: mdi:lightbulb-on\n      &quot;off&quot;: mdi:lightbulb-outline\n  domain_icons:\n    light: mdi:lightbulb\n</code></pre>\n<p>A theme can re-colour a name but never remove one, so a button already set to\na colour your palette leaves out still draws. A font a theme names but that is\nnot installed falls back to the default font until you add the file, exactly\nas it does when you pick one yourself.</p>\n<h4 id=\"one-colour-for-the-whole-remote\">One colour for the whole remote</h4>\n<p>To put everything in a single colour, name a <code>tint:</code> instead of listing nine\ncolours:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">name: Forest\ntint: &quot;2ECC71&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The tint is what icons draw in, and every other colour name becomes a shade of\nit — so a button already set to <code>blue</code> joins in rather than staying the one odd\ncolour on the page. The shades descend in brightness, and <em>unavailable</em> is the\ndimmest of them: with one hue, a dead button can only be told apart by being\ndarker than everything else.</p>\n<p>Anything you spell out under <code>colors:</code> still wins over the tint, so you can\ntake the ramp and pin one colour by hand:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">name: Forest\ntint: &quot;2ECC71&quot;\ncolors:\n  states:\n    unavailable: &quot;EA5A5C&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Always put quotes around a hex colour.</strong> YAML reads an unquoted <code>859900</code> as\na number and an unquoted <code>001122</code> as octal, and in both cases the colour never\narrives — the built-in one is kept and a warning naming the theme and the\ncolour is written to the Home Assistant log.</p>\n<h3 id=\"debug-bug-tab\">Debug (bug tab)</h3>\n<p>The bug tab is where you look when the remote does not answer. <strong>Reachability</strong> pings\nit and says whether it came back. <strong>Traffic</strong> counts every message between Home\nAssistant and the remote since the panel was opened — how many were asked and\nanswered, how many timed out or were lost, and how long the round trip took — with\nthe most recent messages listed underneath, newest first. Nothing here changes the\nremote; it only reports.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reporting-a-problem\">Reporting a problem</h3>\n<p>Every screen of the panel has a <strong>⋮</strong> button — in the toolbar, and on each device in\nthe list. It offers two downloads and the way back:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The ⋮ menu — two downloads and the way back\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/reporting-a-problem.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Download diagnostics</strong> — everything a bug report needs, in one ZIP you can attach\n  to a mail. There is nothing to assemble by hand and nothing to copy out of a log.</li>\n<li><strong>Download configuration</strong> — a copy of your pages, buttons, action library,\n  settings and your own themes, byte for byte as they are stored. No logs, nothing\n  about your network, and nothing added: this is the one to keep as a backup, to\n  compare against another remote, or to send when the question is about a template.\n  It arrives at once, with no window to watch.</li>\n<li><strong>Restore configuration</strong> — reads such a ZIP back in, so a configuration that was\n  lost does not have to be built a second time. See below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ⋮ is the only place these live; there is no button in the gear tab.</p>\n<p>While diagnostics are being collected, a window lists each part and ticks it off when\nit lands, grouped by where it comes from: your configuration, the remote, Home\nAssistant, the logs. Collecting takes a few seconds, most of it spent waiting on the\nremote — which is why each of its requests is a line of its own, so you can see which\none is being waited on. You can close the window and carry on; collecting continues,\nthe download still starts on its own, and a small marker in the corner shows how far\nit has come and brings the window back when you click it. When the bundle is ready\nthe download starts and the window closes itself, with a short message naming the\nfile.</p>\n<p>Asking for diagnostics twice does not start a second collection — it brings the\nrunning one's window back, and the ⋮ says how far it has come. A configuration\ndownload can be started at any time, including while diagnostics are still running.</p>\n<p>If something goes wrong, the window says what and offers <strong>Try again</strong>. Closing it\nputs the matter away and the ⋮ offers the download again as usual. Closing the panel\nor reloading the page stops a collection in progress — the bundle would have had\nnobody to go to — so start it again when you come back.</p>\n<p>The diagnostics ZIP contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the configuration of this remote: its pages, what each button is bound to, and the\n  action library — both as it is read and as the YAML files sit on disk;</li>\n<li>every setting that is not a page: the device entry's own data and options, the\n  fonts installed, the icon rendering settings, the custom icon sets in use, and the\n  model's blueprint and available templates;</li>\n<li>the remote's own report — its read-only endpoints, its log, which modules log at\n  which level, and whether its HTTP API answered at all;</li>\n<li>the messages recently exchanged with the remote, including their content;</li>\n<li>the lines from Home Assistant's log that mention lizaip, plus the errors and\n  warnings Home Assistant is currently holding;</li>\n<li>this remote's entities with their current state, the versions of Home Assistant,\n  the integration and Python, every other custom integration installed, and how long\n  this integration took to set up.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ZIP unpacks into one folder. The four logs sit at the top, next to a\n<code>summary.txt</code> that answers the first questions in a few lines — which remote, whether\nits HTTP API answered, how large the configuration is, which versions are in play and\nwhat could not be collected:</p>\n<pre><code>lizaip-diagnostics-…/\n  summary.txt           the one-page overview\n  README.txt            what every file is, and what is in it about your home\n  manifest.json         what was collected, and the reason for anything that was not\n  log_homeassistant.txt Home Assistant's log, the lines that mention lizaip\n  log_device.txt        the remote's own log, as it reports it\n  log_messages.json     the messages exchanged with the remote, with their content\n  log_errors.json       the errors and warnings Home Assistant is holding\n  remote/               the remote's own answers: report, per-endpoint output,\n                        reachability and log levels\n  homeassistant/        this remote's entities, the versions in play, and the device\n                        page's own report\n  configuration/        the stored configuration, every setting, and yaml/ — the\n                        configuration files exactly as they sit on disk\n</code></pre>\n<p>A configuration download is a different shape: a folder named after the device id,\nholding only the YAML files. <strong>Restore configuration</strong> reads it back in; dropping that\nfolder into <code>&lt;config&gt;/lizaip/</code> by hand still works as well. There is no README and no\nsummary — there is nothing to explain.</p>\n<p><strong>Please read before sending.</strong> The bundle names the entities your buttons act on —\nyour lights, media players and scripts, by their entity ids, both in the configuration\nand in the messages exchanged with the remote. That is what makes a button doing the\nwrong thing diagnosable, and it is the one part of the file that says anything about\nyour home. The Home Assistant log inside it is filtered to lines mentioning lizaip, so\nother integrations' messages are not included. A <code>README.txt</code> inside the ZIP repeats\nall of this.</p>\n<p>If the remote is offline the download still works: the parts that could not be\ncollected are listed in <code>manifest.json</code> with the reason, and \"the remote did not\nanswer\" is frequently the most useful thing in the file.</p>\n<p>The device page (<strong>Settings → Devices &amp; Services → lizaIP → <em>device name</em> → ⋮ →\nDownload diagnostics</strong>) has Home Assistant's own diagnostics button as well. That one\nproduces a JSON file rather than the ZIP. It now carries nearly everything the ZIP\ndoes — the settings, the fonts and icons, the remote's report and log levels, the\nerrors Home Assistant holds, and the lizaip lines from its log — and it opens with a\n<code>summary</code> block that says whether the remote was answering and which parts of the file\nwere shortened to keep it readable in a browser. It is written to be safe to paste in\npublic: your entity ids are left out of the configuration and blanked out of the log\nlines (<code>light.kitchen_ceiling</code> appears as <code>light.***</code>), and the content of the messages\nexchanged with the remote is not included.</p>\n<p>Send the ZIP from the panel if you were asked for diagnostics; it is the complete one.\nThe JSON is the one to attach to a public issue.</p>\n<h3 id=\"restoring-a-configuration\">Restoring a configuration</h3>\n<p><strong>⋮ → Restore configuration</strong> opens one window that walks the whole job in three\nsteps — <strong>Choose</strong>, <strong>Check</strong>, <strong>Restore</strong> — named across the top so you can see how\nmuch is left. The first asks for the file: choose it, or drop the ZIP onto the\nwindow, and a bar shows it going up. It is the\none ZIP <strong>Download configuration</strong> saved: a configuration is many YAML files, but\nthey travel together inside that single archive, so that is all there is to hand\nback. Both shapes are accepted: a configuration download, and a full diagnostics\nbundle, which carries the same YAML files under <code>configuration/yaml/</code>, so a bundle\nyou sent in is also one you can come back from.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The first step — choosing the ZIP to restore from\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/restore-config.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>The file is read and checked before anything is touched: it has to be a ZIP, it has\nto hold lizaIP's YAML, and every file in it has to parse. If it does not, the window\nsays why and stays open for another file; nothing has changed. What passes takes you\nto the second step: a card with the file's name, the date it was downloaded and how\nmany pages it holds, and the pages themselves drawn in the order the remote shows\nthem — the real faces, with the icons of the buttons on them, under the page's\ntitle and its number of buttons. An archive from another remote is taken without\ncomment: restoring across remotes is allowed, and the pages are simply taken\nover. <strong>Back</strong> returns you to the picker, and\nnothing has been written until you press <strong>Restore</strong>.</p>\n<p>A restore <strong>replaces</strong> — it does not merge. Every page, button and the action library\nof that remote are the archive's afterwards; anything created since is gone, and no\ncopy of it is kept. <strong>Download configuration</strong> first if there is anything in the\ncurrent one you may want back.</p>\n<p>Themes are the one exception, because they are shared by every remote: the archive's\nthemes are added beside the device folders, and one you already have under the same\nname is left exactly as it is.</p>\n<p>Nothing is half-written on the way there. The new configuration is written into a\nfolder beside the live one and only then moved into place, so a restore that cannot\nfinish leaves the old configuration where it was rather than a mixture of the two.</p>\n<p>The last step holds the window while the files are written and the panel re-reads\nthem, ticking off each of those two as it passes them, and then says how many pages\ncame back and waits for you to close it. Closing it puts the keyboard back on the\npanel rather than on the ⋮ the window was opened from. The remote is rebuilt in the\nbackground rather than being waited for: pushing a whole configuration back takes a\ndevice round trip per page, and none of it is anything the panel reads, so the\nrestore is finished on screen while the remote is still catching up on its own.</p>\n<p>Afterwards the panel reads the remote from scratch — pages, buttons, thumbnails and\nthe open editor all come from the restored configuration rather than from what was on\nscreen a moment ago — and the remote is sent the whole thing anew, so its screen\nmatches without a restart. If the remote is offline the restore still happens — Home\nAssistant holds the configuration either way — and it catches up when it is back.</p>\n<p>What a configuration download does <strong>not</strong> carry: the remote's connection settings,\nthe icon rendering options, the fonts you uploaded and who the remote is assigned to.\nThose belong to the device entry rather than to the pages, and a restore leaves them\nexactly as they are.</p>\n<h3 id=\"letting-somebody-else-configure-a-remote\">Letting somebody else configure a remote</h3>\n<p>By default only a Home Assistant administrator sees the lizaIP panel. If you want the\nperson who actually uses a remote to set up its own pages and buttons without making\nthem an administrator of your whole house, assign the remote to them: open the <strong>⋮</strong>\nof the remote and pick <strong>Assign user…</strong>. The window lists the active users without\nadministrator rights; switch on the ones who should configure this remote and press\n<strong>Save</strong>.</p>\n<p>Administrators always have access to every remote, so they are not listed. Somebody\nwith no login of their own cannot be assigned — give them a Home Assistant user\naccount first. Until there is at least one active user without administrator rights,\n<strong>Assign user…</strong> is greyed out.</p>\n<p>What changes for an assigned user:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>lizaIP</strong> item appears in their sidebar. While somebody is assigned no remote\n  at all, the item is hidden from them instead, so the panel is not something they\n  have to ignore.</li>\n<li>They see only the remotes assigned to them — their panel's remote list is their\n  own, and so is everything they can reach from it.</li>\n<li>They can do everything to those remotes that you can: pages, buttons, actions,\n  images, the per-remote settings and the remote's own theme.</li>\n<li>They can download a configuration or a diagnostics bundle for their own remote and\n  restore one onto it — it is their remote to look after. A bundle they build\n  describes that remote rather than your installation: the log is filtered down to\n  it, the error log every integration shares is left out, and the environment report\n  keeps the versions without your configuration path or your list of custom\n  integrations. The bundle's own README says so, so nobody reads a narrowed one as a\n  complete one. If you need the whole picture, build the bundle yourself.</li>\n<li>They cannot add or remove a remote, use the debug tab, change the shared theme\n  library, or see who else a remote is assigned to. Those stay with administrators.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Taking a remote away works the same way and takes effect at once: if their panel is\nopen when you save, the remote disappears from it without a reload.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>This scopes the panel, not your house.</strong> It decides which remotes somebody can\nconfigure — it does not narrow what those buttons may then do. An assigned user\nputting an action on a button can target any entity their own Home Assistant\naccount is allowed to call a service on, exactly as they could from a dashboard.\nHome Assistant's own user and entity permissions are unchanged by this, and are\nstill the thing that decides what anyone can actually switch on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-use-mdi-text-media\">How to use mdi, text, media</h2>\n<p>A button's <strong>Icon</strong>, its <strong>Tooltip</strong> and a page's <strong>title</strong> are edited the same way\nand accept the same values: one plain text box, so anything in it can be copied and\npasted into another field. It accepts one of these schemes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scheme</th>\n<th>Shows</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mdi:&lt;icon-name&gt;</code></td>\n<td>A <a href=\"https://pictogrammers.com/library/mdi/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Material Design Icon</a></td>\n<td><code>mdi:lightbulb</code>, <code>mdi:page-next</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;set&gt;:&lt;icon-name&gt;</code></td>\n<td>An icon from any icon set a dashboard plugin registered in Home Assistant, such as <a href=\"https://github.com/elax46/custom-brand-icons\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">custom-brand-icons</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/arallsopp/hass-hue-icons\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">hass-hue-icons</a></td>\n<td><code>phu:ceiling-fan</code>, <code>hue:bulb-group</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>logo:&lt;name&gt;</code></td>\n<td>A bundled brand logo or one of your own, in its native colours</td>\n<td><code>logo:sonos</code>, <code>logo:netflix</code>, <code>logo:my_logo</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>text:&lt;content&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Plain text, rendered as an image. Plain words need no prefix: <code>Kitchen</code> is stored as you typed it and drawn as text</td>\n<td><code>text:22°C</code>, <code>Kitchen</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>media:&lt;url-or-path&gt;</code></td>\n<td>A live thumbnail — album art, a camera snapshot, anything HA can fetch. Takes <a href=\"#options-for-a-media-picture\">options</a> such as <code>?size=200x200</code></td>\n<td><code>media:${media_player.living_room.entity_picture}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>history:&lt;entity-id&gt;</code></td>\n<td>A graph of an entity's recent history. <code>?hours=</code> or <code>?days=</code> sets how far back it looks</td>\n<td><code>history:sensor.outdoor_temperature</code>, <code>history:sensor.house_power?days=7</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>http(s)://…</code>, <code>/local/…</code></td>\n<td>A picture from a URL or from Home Assistant's <code>www</code> folder</td>\n<td><code>/local/kitchen.png</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Editing works the same in all three fields:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Enter, or leaving the field, saves it.</strong> Escape undoes the edit.</li>\n<li><strong>A suggestion list opens once you type an icon prefix</strong> Home Assistant knows —\n  <code>mdi:</code>, plus any icon set a dashboard plugin registered, such as <code>phu:</code>.\n  Typing <code>logo:</code> lists bundled logos and your own files.\n  Click a suggestion, or pick one\n  with the arrow keys and Enter, and it is saved straight away. For anything else\n  (words, a URL) no list gets in the way.</li>\n<li><strong>In a title naming several things, the list follows the caret.</strong> Typing\n  <code>logo:hue mdi:li</code> suggests lightbulbs for the part being typed, and picking one\n  replaces only that part and leaves the field open, because a title of several\n  parts is not finished by a single pick. Move the caret back into an earlier part\n  to complete that one instead. A field holding one thing is still saved\n  straight away.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A plain <code>mdi:</code> icon, as seen on an actual button here (next to the Sonos pages, this one\nuses <code>mdi:page-next</code> for \"go to the next page\"):</p>\n<p><img alt=\"An mdi: icon button, next to logo: pages\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/panel-overview.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>Notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An icon set needs its plugin.</strong> A <code>&lt;set&gt;:</code> name only works if that set's\n  dashboard plugin is installed; without it the request falls back to nothing\n  (a blank icon).</li>\n<li><strong>Icon sets reach the remote through the editor.</strong> Their shapes live in\n  the browser (that is where the plugin runs), so the editor hands each one to\n  the integration as you save it. A name a\n  <a href=\"#how-to-use-variables-with-examples\">variable</a>\n  makes up at run time (<code>hue:battery-${…}</code>) is fetched the next time you open\n  the editor; until then the remote shows nothing for it.</li>\n<li><strong><code>logo:</code> includes bundled brands and your own files.</strong> Type <code>logo:</code> to see\n  the available names, including <code>hue</code>, <code>netflix</code>, <code>sonos</code> and <code>spotify</code>.\n  Add a colour (<code>logo:sonos?fg=white</code>) to render an SVG monochrome instead of in\n  its original colours. See <a href=\"#adding-your-own-logos\">Adding your own logos</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>text:</code> always keeps room for accents and tails.</strong> The image is cut to the highest\n  any letter of the font can reach (<code>Ä</code>) and the lowest (<code>g</code>), whether or not the text\n  contains such letters, so two short labels of different shape (<code>Hi</code> vs <code>Hg</code>) stay the\n  same height, stand on the same line, and a row of text buttons doesn't jitter.</li>\n<li><strong>A space puts things next to each other</strong> — <code>logo:sonos Küche</code> draws the brand\n  mark <em>and</em> the room. See\n  <a href=\"#combining-several-things-in-one-picture\">Combining several things in one picture</a>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>media:</code></strong> is how live thumbnails — a Sonos page's album art, for instance — get\n  onto a button; it is usually filled in automatically by a template page rather than\n  typed by hand. To scale it or change how it is drawn, see\n  <a href=\"#options-for-a-media-picture\">Options for a <code>media:</code> picture</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"adding-your-own-logos\">Adding your own logos</h3>\n<p>Logos go beside your custom fonts: fonts live in <code>/config/www/fonts/</code>, and\nlogos in <strong><code>/config/www/logos/</code></strong>. If your Home Assistant configuration directory\nis elsewhere, use its <code>www/logos/</code> folder instead.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create <code>www/logos/</code> inside your Home Assistant configuration directory if it\n   does not exist.</li>\n<li>Copy an SVG or PNG into it, for example <code>my_logo.png</code>. Keep files directly in\n   this folder, not in subfolders.</li>\n<li>In a button's <strong>Icon</strong>, its <strong>Tooltip</strong>, or a page's <strong>title</strong>, type\n   <code>logo:my_logo</code>, or start with <code>logo:</code> and choose it from the suggestions.\n   Leave off the filename extension.</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">/config/www/\n  fonts/\n  logos/\n    my_logo.png\n    my_logo_wide.png\n</code></pre>\n<p>New files are available without restarting Home Assistant and survive integration\nupdates. Names are case-sensitive; use ASCII letters, digits, <code>_</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>+</code> or\n<code>.</code> without spaces, a leading dot or <code>..</code>. Use lowercase <code>.svg</code> and <code>.png</code>\nextensions. A transparent background usually works best.</p>\n<p><strong>Colours and shapes.</strong> PNG files keep their own colours. An SVG can be drawn\nmonochrome with <code>logo:my_logo?fg=white</code>; full-colour SVG rendering requires\n<code>cairosvg</code>, otherwise SVGs fall back to monochrome. An optional\n<code>my_logo_wide.png</code> or <code>my_logo_wide.svg</code> is chosen automatically when its\nproportions fit the canvas better. Write <code>logo:my_logo_wide</code> to select it explicitly.</p>\n<p><strong>Replacing a bundled logo.</strong> A user file with the same name wins over the\nbundled logo, including its whole upright/wide family. Supply any variants you\nwant to keep; missing variants are not borrowed from the bundle. Remove your\noverride files to use the bundled logo again.</p>\n<p><strong>After replacing a file</strong>, the next uncached request reads the new image.\nExisting images can remain cached by clients for up to an hour.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Public files:</strong> Home Assistant also serves these files under\n<code>/local/logos/</code>, just as it serves fonts under <code>/local/fonts/</code>. Do not put\nprivate images in this directory.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"options-for-a-media-picture\">Options for a <code>media:</code> picture</h3>\n<p>A <code>media:</code> picture is drawn as large as fits the field's size (a tile is 50×52), keeping\nits own proportions: a square cover in a title stays square. Add options after a <code>?</code>,\njoined with <code>&amp;</code>, like any web address:</p>\n<pre><code>media:https://cdn-profiles.tunein.com/s8007/images/logoq.png?t=160780&amp;size=200x200\nmedia:${_device.entity_picture}?size=200x200&amp;bg=black\n</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>size</code></td>\n<td>Draws the picture at exactly that size, in pixels — stretched to it unless <code>fit=contain</code></td>\n<td><code>size=200x200</code>, <code>size=200</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fit</code></td>\n<td><code>contain</code> keeps the picture's proportions inside the size, <code>fill</code> stretches it to the size. Without a <code>size</code> it is <code>contain</code>, with one <code>fill</code></td>\n<td><code>fit=contain</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>box</code></td>\n<td>Answers with one piece of it: <code>x,y,w,h</code> of the sized picture</td>\n<td><code>box=60,62,60,62</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mode</code></td>\n<td><code>dark</code> (the default) or <code>light</code> background rendering</td>\n<td><code>mode=light</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fg</code></td>\n<td>Tint colour, for a monochrome picture</td>\n<td><code>fg=white</code>, <code>fg=FF8800</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bg</code></td>\n<td>Background colour behind the picture</td>\n<td><code>bg=black</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>crop</code></td>\n<td><code>0</code> keeps the padding around the picture</td>\n<td><code>crop=0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>alpha</code></td>\n<td><code>1</code> keeps the picture's transparency</td>\n<td><code>alpha=1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>percent</code></td>\n<td>Fill level <code>0</code>–<code>100</code>: the lower part stays solid, the part above is dimmed</td>\n<td><code>percent=40</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These options are taken out of the address before the picture is fetched, so the\npicture's own server never sees them. Everything else in the address goes to that server\nunchanged (<code>t=160780</code> above).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A second <code>?</code> is fine.</strong> A variable such as <code>${_device.entity_picture}</code> usually\n  already ends in a query of its own (<code>…?token=…</code>). Write <code>?</code> anyway; a second one\n  counts as <code>&amp;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Quotes keep an address exactly as written.</strong> If the picture's server uses one of\n  the option names itself — say it takes its own <code>size=</code> — put the address in quotes,\n  <code>\"…\"</code> or <code>'…'</code> (the same at both ends). Everything inside goes to that server; only\n  what follows the closing quote is an option:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>media:'https://host/logo.png?size=2000'?size=200x200&amp;percent=50</code></p>\n<p>Here <code>size=2000</code> is sent to <code>host</code>, and the picture is drawn at 200×200, half filled.\n- <strong><code>size</code> takes pixels or a name.</strong> <code>200x200</code>, <code>200</code>, or one of the sizes the\n  remote itself uses: <code>tile</code> (50×52), <code>title</code> (200×50), <code>tooltip</code> (200×40). Anything\n  else — <code>size=large</code> — is not an option and goes to the picture's server as part of\n  the address.\n- <strong>A <code>percent</code> written here wins</strong> over the fill level the button would show on its\n  own (a lamp's brightness, for instance).\n- <strong><code>box</code> spreads one picture over several buttons.</strong> See\n  <a href=\"#spreading-one-picture-over-several-buttons\">Spreading one picture over several buttons</a>.\n- <strong>The options work on any picture, not only <code>media:</code>.</strong> <code>mdi:battery?size=tile</code>,\n  <code>logo:hue?fg=white</code>, and the words a variable resolves to:\n  <code>${sensor.kitchen_battery}?size=tile</code> draws the battery level at tile size. Words\n  that merely end in a question mark stay a question — only a <code>?</code> followed by at\n  least one option is read as one. Anything in that query the remote does not\n  know — a typo such as <code>?size=tile&amp;1rea</code> — is dropped, and the options beside it\n  still apply.\n- <strong>Words are made to fit the size asked for.</strong> \"91 %\" at tile size is drawn\n  smaller so all of it shows, rather than running off the side. The lettering is\n  only ever made smaller, never bigger, so two tiles beside each other keep the\n  same size, and it stops where it would become unreadable. Write a <code>font_size=</code> to\n  say the size yourself — words wider than their strip are then left wide, which is\n  what lets the remote scroll a long page title.</p>\n<p><strong>Colour names.</strong> <code>fg=</code> and <code>bg=</code> take a 6- or 8-digit hex (<code>FF8800</code>, <code>FF880080</code>) or one\nof these names: <code>white</code>, <code>black</code>, <code>yellow</code>, <code>blue</code>, <code>green</code>, <code>red</code>, <code>inactive</code>,\n<code>transparent</code> — plus four named for a <em>state</em> rather than a hue, for tinting a tile by\nwhat the thing behind it is doing:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>Draws</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fg=on</code></td>\n<td>plain white</td>\n<td>on, running, open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fg=off</code></td>\n<td>a grey</td>\n<td>off, idle, closed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fg=unavailable</code></td>\n<td>a dimmed red</td>\n<td>not answering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fg=default</code></td>\n<td>plain white</td>\n<td>nothing to say: no entity, or an action like Next track</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>on</code> is simply the default foreground, white: a tile that is on is the normal case,\nand a page reads calmer when only the departures from normal carry a hue. <code>unavailable</code>\nis deliberately duller than <code>red</code>: an entity that has gone missing is not an alarm, and\nat alarm strength every dead tile shouts louder than the one thing that is actually\nwrong. The three are not offered as one-click swatches in the panel's colour picker —\neach draws a colour already on offer there — but they are accepted wherever a colour is\ntyped.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spreading-one-picture-over-several-buttons\">Spreading one picture over several buttons</h3>\n<p>One picture can cover a block of buttons: each button carries the same address and\ntakes a different piece of it. Two options do the work — <code>size</code> scales the whole\npicture, <code>box</code> says which piece to show:</p>\n<pre><code>media:/local/art.png?size=180x186&amp;box=60,62,60,62\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>box</code> is four numbers, <code>x,y,w,h</code>: <code>x,y</code> is where the piece starts, counted from the\ntop left of the scaled picture, and <code>w,h</code> is how big it is.</p>\n<p><strong>Laying out a grid.</strong> Decide the tile size first, then multiply:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A tile on the remote is 60×62. For a 3×3 block, scale the picture to\n   3 × 60 = 180 wide and 3 × 62 = 186 high: <code>?size=180x186</code>.</li>\n<li>The tile in column <em>c</em>, row <em>r</em> (both counted from 0) starts at\n   <code>x = c × 60</code>, <code>y = r × 62</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Column 0</th>\n<th>Column 1</th>\n<th>Column 2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Row 0</strong></td>\n<td><code>box=0,0,60,62</code></td>\n<td><code>box=60,0,60,62</code></td>\n<td><code>box=120,0,60,62</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Row 1</strong></td>\n<td><code>box=0,62,60,62</code></td>\n<td><code>box=60,62,60,62</code></td>\n<td><code>box=120,62,60,62</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Row 2</strong></td>\n<td><code>box=0,124,60,62</code></td>\n<td><code>box=60,124,60,62</code></td>\n<td><code>box=120,124,60,62</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The same works for any block — two buttons side by side are <code>?size=120x62</code> with\n<code>box=0,0,60,62</code> and <code>box=60,0,60,62</code>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Sonos Cover</strong> template is this grid ready-made: the playing track's cover across\nbuttons 1–3, 5–7 and 9–11, each tile <code>${_device.entity_picture}?size=180x186&amp;box=…</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Good to know</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The piece always comes back in the size the box named.</strong> Where it reaches past\n  the picture it is empty rather than short, so a picture whose size does not divide\n  evenly still gives a grid that lines up.</li>\n<li><strong>The piece is never trimmed to what is drawn on it.</strong> A tile that is mostly empty\n  keeps its size, so it stays in line with the tiles beside it.</li>\n<li><strong>A <code>bg=</code> fills the piece</strong>, not the picture it was cut from.</li>\n<li><strong>The pieces are separate pictures to the remote</strong>, each cached under its own\n  address — changing one button's box leaves the others alone.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not only for <code>media:</code>.</strong> Any picture takes a box, so <code>/local/art.png</code> or a\n  variable resolving to an address works the same way.</li>\n<li><strong><code>box</code> needs four whole numbers.</strong> Anything else is not read as an option, and\n  goes to the picture's own server as part of the address.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"combining-several-things-in-one-picture\">Combining several things in one picture</h3>\n<p>An Image, a tooltip or a page title may name several things separated by spaces; they\nare drawn side by side in one picture, in the order written.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>The remote shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>logo:sonos Küche</code></td>\n<td>the Sonos mark, then the word \"Küche\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>logo:sonos - Küche</code>, <code>logo:sonos + Küche</code></td>\n<td>the same — a lone <code>-</code> or <code>+</code> between parts is only for looks and is dropped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mdi:lightbulb Wohnzimmer</code></td>\n<td>a bulb, then \"Wohnzimmer\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>logo:hue text:Hue mdi:lightbulb</code></td>\n<td>the Hue mark, the word \"Hue\" and a bulb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>phu:ceiling-fan text:Fan</code></td>\n<td>the fan icon, then \"Fan\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>logo:sonos ${_device}</code></td>\n<td>the Sonos mark, then the name of the page's device</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.entity_picture} + logo:sonos + ${_device.friendly_name}</code></td>\n<td>the cover now playing, the Sonos mark and the player's name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mdi:thermometer ${sensor.outdoor_temperature}</code></td>\n<td>a thermometer, then \"21.5 °C\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mdi:play mdi:pause</code></td>\n<td>two icons sharing the space</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Where a part starts.</strong> A word shaped like a source — <code>mdi:</code>, <code>logo:</code>, <code>text:</code>,\n<code>media:</code>, <code>phu:</code> or any other icon set — always starts a new part. After <code>logo:</code> and\n<code>mdi:</code> (whose names never contain a space) plain words start a <code>text:</code> part on their\nown, so <code>logo:sonos Küche</code> needs no <code>text:</code>. After anything else, spell the words out\nwith <code>text:</code>: <code>phu:ceiling-fan Fan</code> would be read as one icon named \"ceiling-fan Fan\",\nand a picture — <code>media:</code>, an HA path such as <code>/local/a.png</code>, or an <code>http(s)://</code> address —\nends at the first space (write a space inside an address as <code>%20</code>). Words that follow a <code>text:</code> part belong to it,\nso <code>text:Living room</code> is one phrase. A variable is replaced before the picture is\nsplit, so a device name with spaces stays one name.</p>\n<p><strong>How the parts are laid out.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The words always keep room for accents and tails.</strong> A picture with words is as tall\n  as the font can reach above and below the line the letters sit on, whether or not\n  the name has an <code>Ä</code> or a <code>g</code>, so titles and buttons line up.</li>\n<li><strong>Icons and logos stand on that baseline and are as tall as the tallest letter</strong>\n  beside them, plus one pixel above and one below so they do not look smaller than the\n  letters: next to <code>Wohnzimmer</code> a bulb is as tall as the <code>W</code>, next to <code>wasser</code> only as\n  tall as the lower-case letters.</li>\n<li><strong>Beside a logo or icon the words have a fixed size</strong> (32 px in a page title, or the\n  title font size if that is smaller), so a name looks the same next to any mark. A row\n  wider than the title bar is simply wider, and the remote scrolls it; only a very long\n  name is cut short with <code>…</code>. Words on their own still shrink to fit the bar, down to\n  32 px.</li>\n<li><strong>On a square button</strong> a single mark followed by words is stacked instead: the mark\n  on top, the words below, shrunk and cut short with <code>…</code> to fit the width. Anything\n  else — three parts, or the words first — is drawn in a row.</li>\n<li><strong>Marks without words</strong> (<code>mdi:play mdi:pause</code>) share the space evenly and are\n  centred.</li>\n<li><strong>Colour.</strong> The page or button colour applies to the words and to the icons; a\n  <code>logo:</code> beside other parts keeps its brand colours.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For the full technical reference — query parameters, available fonts, icon tinting —\nsee the\n<a href=\"https://github.com/liza-home/home-assistant/blob/main/custom_components/lizaip/README.md#image-server-imgserv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Image Server section of the developer README</a>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-use-variables-with-examples\">How to use variables with examples</h2>\n<p>A button's <strong>Tooltip</strong>, a button's <strong>Icon</strong>, and a page <strong>title</strong> can all show the\ncurrent value of any Home Assistant entity, by writing its entity ID inside <code>${…}</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>${sensor.outdoor_temperature}        → 21.5 °C   (state, with its unit)\n${weather.home.temperature}          → 18        (one attribute, no unit)\n${weather.forecast_home}             → Partly cloudy (a weather condition, in your language)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Worked examples</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>The remote shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip</td>\n<td><code>Outside ${sensor.outdoor_temperature}</code></td>\n<td>Outside 21.5 °C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip</td>\n<td><code>Battery ${sensor.lizaip_1c63499a7193_battery}</code></td>\n<td>Battery 85 %</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip</td>\n<td><code>${media_player.living_room.media_title}</code></td>\n<td>the song that is playing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon</td>\n<td><code>text:${weather.home.temperature}°</code></td>\n<td>18°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon</td>\n<td><code>mdi:battery ${sensor.lizaip_1c63499a7193_battery}</code></td>\n<td>a battery mark beside 85 %</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon</td>\n<td><code>${weather.forecast_home}</code></td>\n<td>the current weather as an icon (<code>mdi:weather-partly-cloudy</code>, …)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip</td>\n<td><code>Today ${weather.forecast_home}</code></td>\n<td>Today Partly cloudy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon</td>\n<td><code>mdi:${sensor.lizaip_battery_icon}</code></td>\n<td>an icon chosen by a template helper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Page title</td>\n<td><code>Living room ${sensor.living_room_temperature}</code></td>\n<td>Living room 22 °C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Page title (template page)</td>\n<td><code>${_device}</code></td>\n<td>the name of the device (or hub) the page is bound to</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Page title (template page)</td>\n<td><code>Vol ${round(_device.volume_level * 100)}%</code></td>\n<td>Vol 12%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip (Sonos template page)</td>\n<td><code>${_device.media_title_prev}</code></td>\n<td>Endless Summer — the previous track (see <a href=\"#track-names-current-previous-next\">Track names</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip (template page)</td>\n<td><code>${_device} ${round(_device.volume_level * 100)}%</code></td>\n<td>Living Room TV 12%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip (Hue template page)</td>\n<td><code>${_device} ${_device.sw_version}</code></td>\n<td>Hue Bridge 1.56.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip (template page)</td>\n<td><code>${_device} · ${_device.area_name}</code></td>\n<td>Living Room TV · Living Room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip (page you made yourself, on a play/pause button)</td>\n<td><code>${_device.media_title}</code></td>\n<td>the song the button's player is playing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>On a template page, <code>${_device}</code> is always the page's own device.</strong> Whatever page it is written on,\nand whichever field — the title, a button's image, its tooltip, an action's icon —\nit means the one device that page is bound to: the Sonos speaker, the Android TV,\nthe Hue bridge. It never means the light a particular button happens to switch.</p>\n<p><strong>On a page you made yourself</strong>, which is bound to nothing, a button's\n<code>${_device…}</code> means what that button controls: on a play/pause button for the\nliving-room speaker, <code>${_device.media_title}</code> is the song it plays and\n<code>${_device}</code> the speaker's name. A button that controls nothing reads nothing.</p>\n<p>One rule decides where each one is read from: <strong>what only the device knows\ncomes from the device; everything an entity can answer comes from the\nentity.</strong></p>\n<p>These are facts about the device itself — nothing but the device knows them —\nand they answer on <em>every</em> template page:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>Shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.area_name}</code></td>\n<td>the room it is filed under, <code>Office</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.friendly_name}</code></td>\n<td>its name, <code>Living Room</code> — the same as <code>${_device}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.label_names}</code></td>\n<td>the labels stuck on it, <code>Favourites, Test</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.manufacturer}</code></td>\n<td>who made it, <code>Signify</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.model}</code></td>\n<td>the model, <code>BSB002</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.sw_version}</code></td>\n<td>the firmware it runs, <code>1.56.0</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Home Assistant leaves rooms unassigned unless you set them, so\n<code>${_device.area_name}</code> is often blank. Set one under <strong>Settings → Areas</strong> and\nit fills in. Where a single entity is filed somewhere other than its device,\nthe entity's room wins.</p>\n<p>A <strong>room is also where a zone from your lights lands</strong>: a Hue room or zone is\nimported by Home Assistant as an area, so <code>${_device.area_name}</code> is what names\nit. A Sonos zone is the speaker itself, so that is <code>${_device}</code>. A Home\nAssistant <strong>zone</strong> is a different thing again — a circle drawn on a map, which\na phone moves in and out of — and nothing a remote page is bound to can be in\none, so there is no token for it.</p>\n<p><code>${_device.label_names}</code> reads the labels you set under <strong>Settings → Areas,\nlabels &amp; zones → Labels</strong>. Several are listed alphabetically, separated by\ncommas. A label on the entity itself wins over one on its device. It is\nplural, and spelled apart from Home Assistant's own <code>label_name</code>, which takes\none label and gives back its name.</p>\n<p>Everything else — <code>${_device.volume_level}</code>, <code>${_device.source}</code>,\n<code>${_device.media_title}</code> — is an <strong>attribute of the page's entity</strong>. A page bound\nto a hub has no entity of its own (a bridge is not a thing with a state), so\nattributes stay blank there while the facts above still answer: on a Hue page\n<code>${_device}</code> names the bridge and <code>${_device.sw_version}</code> gives its firmware.</p>\n<p>Type <code>${_device.</code> in any of these fields and the editor lists them with\nwhat each one says beside it, so you can see that <code>model</code> is <code>BSB002</code> before\nyou pick it. They are listed alphabetically. A fact the integration never\nfilled in is listed with nothing beside it — which is what it will draw.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-name\">The name</h3>\n<p><code>${_device}</code> is the name of the device the page is bound to. It reads the same\non every page — a Sonos page, a Hue page, any other — so the same thing\nwritten on two pages answers the same way.</p>\n<p><code>${_device.friendly_name}</code> is the same name written out in full, and is\noffered in the list of facts above beside the room and the model. Use\nwhichever reads better: <code>${_device}</code> where the line is already crowded,\n<code>${_device.friendly_name}</code> where it sits next to its neighbours and you want\nto see at a glance what it is.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>Reads</th>\n<th>On a Sonos page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device}</code></td>\n<td>the device's name</td>\n<td>Living Room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.friendly_name}</code></td>\n<td>the same, written out</td>\n<td>Living Room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${media_player.kueche.friendly_name}</code></td>\n<td>that entity's own name</td>\n<td>Living Room Media Player</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An entity can be named differently from its device — rename the player alone\nand the two part ways. When you want the entity's name rather than the\ndevice's, name the entity in full, as the last row does.</p>\n<p>The device's name is the one the user gave it, then the integration's. A hub\nwhose bridge cannot be found falls back to its config entry's title, which is\na label over the integration and often carries a serial (<code>Hue Bridge\necb5fa2ae5d6</code>).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Either way the name is the page's own. To name some <em>other</em> entity, write it\nout in full: <code>${media_player.kitchen.friendly_name}</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"track-names-current-previous-next\">Track names: current, previous, next</h3>\n<p>On a page built around a media player (a template page such as Sonos), these name the\ntracks. Type <code>${_device.</code> in a tooltip and the editor offers them.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>Shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.media_title}</code> / <code>${_device.media_artist}</code></td>\n<td>the track playing now, its artist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.media_title_prev}</code> / <code>${_device.media_artist_prev}</code></td>\n<td>the track before it (where <strong>Previous</strong> goes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_device.media_title_next}</code> / <code>${_device.media_artist_next}</code></td>\n<td>the track after it (where <strong>Next</strong> goes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${_state} - ${_device.media_title}</code></td>\n<td>Pause - Bohemian Rhapsody</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Previous and next are Sonos only</strong> — no other kind of player tells Home Assistant\n  what is in its queue. For each Sonos speaker a button or template page points at,\n  lizaIP keeps a sensor named after it, <code>sensor.&lt;speaker&gt;_queue</code> (for\n  <code>media_player.kitchen</code>: <code>sensor.kitchen_queue</code>), with <code>next_song</code>, <code>next_artist</code>,\n  <code>prev_song</code> and <code>prev_artist</code>. You can use it anywhere, like any sensor:\n  <code>${sensor.kitchen_queue.next_song}</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>On any page</strong>, <code>${_queue.next_song}</code>, <code>${_queue.next_artist}</code>, <code>${_queue.prev_song}</code>\n  and <code>${_queue.prev_artist}</code> read the queue of the speaker the <em>button</em> points at,\n  rather than the page's player.</li>\n<li><strong>Empty rather than wrong.</strong> With shuffle on, on radio or line-in, or at either end of\n  a queue that does not repeat, there is nothing honest to name, and the value is\n  empty — and so is a tooltip that holds nothing else. With <strong>repeat one</strong>, the next\n  track is the one playing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"words-in-the-remotes-language\">Words in the remote's language</h3>\n<p><code>{translate: …}</code> writes a word in the language the remote is set to (its <strong>Language</strong>\noption, else Home Assistant's), in a <strong>Tooltip</strong>, an <strong>Icon</strong> or a page <strong>title</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>The remote shows (German)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip</td>\n<td><code>{translate: volume}</code></td>\n<td>Lautstärke</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip</td>\n<td><code>{translate: Volume Up} ${sensor.tv_volume}</code></td>\n<td>Lauter 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon</td>\n<td><code>text:{translate: volume}</code></td>\n<td>Lautstärke, drawn as the tile</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The word inside is the English one or its key — <code>{translate: Volume Up}</code> and\n<code>{translate: volume_up}</code> are the same. It works for the words the integration already\ntranslates for buttons and page titles (Volume, Next, Power, Playlists, …); a word it does\nnot know is shown as you wrote it. Change the remote's language and the words follow.</p>\n<h3 id=\"calculating-with-variables\">Calculating with variables</h3>\n<p>Inside <code>${…}</code> you can calculate with numbers and variables. The result is a plain\nnumber, without a unit, so you add the unit yourself:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>Reads (for the values shown)</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${sensor.power / 1000} kW</code></td>\n<td>1.25 kW (power 1250)</td>\n<td>divide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${sensor.outdoor_temperature * 9 / 5 + 32} °F</code></td>\n<td>70.7 °F (21.5)</td>\n<td><code>* / %</code> before <code>+ -</code>, as usual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${(sensor.a + sensor.b) / 2}</code></td>\n<td>20.5 (19 and 22)</td>\n<td>brackets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${sensor.minutes % 60}</code></td>\n<td>15 (75 minutes)</td>\n<td><code>%</code> is the remainder of a division</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${-sensor.offset}</code></td>\n<td>-3 (3)</td>\n<td>a leading minus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${round(sensor.outdoor_temperature)}°</code></td>\n<td>22° (21.5)</td>\n<td>round to a whole number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${round(sensor.outdoor_temperature, 1)}</code></td>\n<td>21.8 (21.75)</td>\n<td>round to 1 decimal (0–6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${min(sensor.x, 100)}</code>, <code>${max(sensor.x, 0)}</code></td>\n<td>the smaller / larger value</td>\n<td>any number of values, at least two</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${abs(sensor.x - 20)}</code></td>\n<td>1.5 (21.5)</td>\n<td>the distance from 20, without the sign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>${round(light.desk.brightness * 100 / 255)}%</code></td>\n<td>50% (brightness 128)</td>\n<td>an attribute in a calculation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Vol ${round(_device.volume_level * 100)}%</code></td>\n<td>Vol 12%</td>\n<td>the page's own device, on a template page</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Functions:</strong> <code>round(x)</code> or <code>round(x, decimals)</code>, <code>min(…)</code>, <code>max(…)</code>, <code>abs(x)</code>.\n  <code>round</code> rounds half away from zero, the way people do: 2.5 becomes 3, -2.5 becomes\n  -3. Function names are not case-sensitive; other functions (<code>floor</code>, <code>int</code>, …) are\n  not available.</li>\n<li><strong>Operators:</strong> <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>, <code>%</code> and brackets. Without <code>round</code>, a result keeps up\n  to six decimals: <code>${light.desk.brightness * 100 / 255}</code> reads 50.196078.</li>\n<li><strong>Anything that is not a number reads as nothing:</strong> a state like <code>on</code> or <code>eco</code>, an\n  entity that does not exist or is unavailable, a division by zero, or a typo. The\n  calculation then drops out of a tooltip, and an image with it falls back, exactly\n  like any variable without a value.</li>\n<li><strong>Icon names.</strong> Because the result is a bare number, a calculation can pick an icon\n  without a template helper: <code>mdi:battery-${max(10, min(90, round(sensor.battery / 10) * 10))}</code>\n  turns 87 % into <code>mdi:battery-90</code> and keeps it between <code>battery-10</code> and <code>battery-90</code>.\n  For anything more involved — choosing between differently named icons, night\n  variants — use a template helper, as below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>${_device}</code> works the same in a template page's tiles, state icons and tooltips, and in the\nicons of the actions its buttons run. Typing <code>${</code> in a template page's title, image or tooltip field also suggests <code>_device</code>, and after <code>${_device.</code>\nthe attributes of the page's entity. A hub page has no entity, so there only\nthe facts above are suggested.</p>\n<p>The editor itself shows you the live value as you type — the hint line under the field\nreads what the text currently resolves to, exactly as seen here while editing a\ntooltip:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"The tooltip field's hint line shows the live, resolved value\" src=\"{{GUIDE_IMG_BASE}}/variable-hint.png\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>Values stay current (updated within ~2 seconds of a change), missing values are left\nout rather than shown as an error, and units are only included in plain text — not\ninside an icon name, since <code>mdi:battery-80 %</code> isn't a valid icon.</p>\n<h3 id=\"using-a-variable-to-generate-an-icon\">Using a variable to generate an icon</h3>\n<p>Every icon field reads variables the same way — the button's <strong>Icon</strong>, its per-state\n<strong>Button icons</strong>, and the icons of an action (its own icon and one per state, edited in\nthe action editor). The hint line under each field shows what it reads right now. A\nvariable with no value never leaves a broken icon: a per-state icon falls back to the\naction's icon for that state, and that in turn to the icon the action would show anyway.</p>\n<p>A variable can sit inside an <strong>Icon</strong> field, not just a tooltip — <code>${sensor.x}</code> as the\nwhole Image makes the button draw that value as a picture, and <code>${sensor.x}</code> inside an\n<code>mdi:</code> name picks a different icon depending on the value. The catch: an icon name has\nto match an existing icon <em>exactly</em>. Battery icons, for example, only exist in steps of\nten (<code>mdi:battery-10</code> … <code>mdi:battery-90</code>, plus <code>mdi:battery</code> for full and\n<code>mdi:battery-outline</code> for empty), while a battery sensor reports every percent — so\n<code>mdi:battery-${sensor.…_battery}</code> would ask for <code>mdi:battery-87</code>, which doesn't exist\nand falls back to a blank icon.</p>\n<p>The fix is a small Home Assistant <strong>template helper</strong>: an entity whose whole job is to\nturn a raw value into the icon name you actually want, which the button's Icon field\nthen just references.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In Home Assistant open <strong>Settings → Devices &amp; services → Helpers → Create helper →\n   Template → Template a sensor</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Name:</strong> <code>lizaIP battery icon</code> — this creates the entity <code>sensor.lizaip_battery_icon</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>State template</strong> — replace <code>sensor.lizaip_1c63499a7193_battery</code> with your remote's\n   own battery sensor:</li>\n</ol>\n<p><code>jinja\n   {% set b = states('sensor.lizaip_1c63499a7193_battery') | int(-1) %}\n   {% if b &lt; 0 %}android\n   {% elif b &gt;= 95 %}battery\n   {% elif b &lt; 5 %}battery-outline\n   {% else %}battery-{{ ((b / 10) | round(0) | int) * 10 }}\n   {% endif %}</code></p>\n<p>Leave unit, device class and state class empty. The preview at the bottom of the\n   dialog should show something like <code>battery-80</code>. A sensor with no value names\n   <code>android</code> instead, so a missing reading shows a placeholder rather than a blank.\n4. <strong>Submit</strong>, then in the lizaIP editor set the button's <strong>Icon</strong> (or a page title) to</p>\n<p><code>mdi:${sensor.lizaip_battery_icon}</code></p>\n<p>The helper returns the name only, so <code>mdi:</code> goes in front of it. (A helper that\n   returns the whole <code>mdi:battery-80</code> works too — then write\n   <code>${sensor.lizaip_battery_icon}</code> on its own.) Add a tooltip such as\n   <code>Battery ${sensor.lizaip_1c63499a7193_battery}</code> alongside it for the exact number.</p>\n<p>If you prefer YAML, the same helper in <code>configuration.yaml</code> — this version also shows a\ncharging icon while the remote sits in its charger:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">template:\n  - sensor:\n      - name: &quot;lizaIP battery icon&quot;\n        availability: &quot;{{ has_value('sensor.lizaip_1c63499a7193_battery') }}&quot;\n        state: &gt;\n          {% set b = states('sensor.lizaip_1c63499a7193_battery') | int(0) %}\n          {% set charging = is_state('binary_sensor.lizaip_1c63499a7193_charging', 'on') %}\n          {% set icon = 'mdi:battery-charging' if charging else 'mdi:battery' %}\n          {% if b &gt;= 95 %}{{ icon ~ ('-100' if charging else '') }}\n          {% elif b &lt; 5 %}{{ icon }}-outline\n          {% else %}{{ icon }}-{{ [[(b + 5) // 10 * 10, 10] | max, 90] | min }}\n          {% endif %}\n</code></pre>\n<h4 id=\"template-wi-fi-signal-icon\">Template: Wi-Fi signal icon</h4>\n<p>Wi-Fi icons come in four bars (<code>mdi:wifi-strength-1</code> … <code>mdi:wifi-strength-4</code>), plus\n<code>mdi:wifi-strength-outline</code> for no bars and <code>mdi:wifi-strength-off-outline</code> when the\ndevice is offline. A signal sensor (ESPHome's <em>WiFi Signal</em>, a router's per-client\nsensor, …) reports dBm — a negative number, closer to 0 is better — so the helper maps\nit onto bars. Replace <code>sensor.living_room_wifi_signal</code> with your own sensor:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-jinja\">{% set s = states('sensor.living_room_wifi_signal') %}\n{% if s in ['unavailable', 'unknown', 'none', ''] %}mdi:wifi-strength-off-outline\n{% else %}\n  {% set dbm = s | float %}\n  {% if dbm &gt;= -55 %}mdi:wifi-strength-4\n  {% elif dbm &gt;= -67 %}mdi:wifi-strength-3\n  {% elif dbm &gt;= -75 %}mdi:wifi-strength-2\n  {% elif dbm &gt;= -85 %}mdi:wifi-strength-1\n  {% else %}mdi:wifi-strength-outline\n  {% endif %}\n{% endif %}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Name the helper e.g. <code>lizaIP wifi icon</code> (entity <code>sensor.lizaip_wifi_icon</code>) and set the\nbutton's <strong>Icon</strong> to <code>${sensor.lizaip_wifi_icon}</code>; a tooltip of\n<code>${sensor.living_room_wifi_signal}</code> adds the exact reading.</p>\n<p>If your sensor reports signal <strong>quality in %</strong> instead of dBm, replace the inner\n<code>if</code> block with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-jinja\">  {% set q = s | int %}\n  {% if q &gt;= 80 %}mdi:wifi-strength-4\n  {% elif q &gt;= 60 %}mdi:wifi-strength-3\n  {% elif q &gt;= 40 %}mdi:wifi-strength-2\n  {% elif q &gt;= 20 %}mdi:wifi-strength-1\n  {% else %}mdi:wifi-strength-outline\n  {% endif %}\n</code></pre>\n<p>As YAML in <code>configuration.yaml</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">template:\n  - sensor:\n      - name: &quot;lizaIP wifi icon&quot;\n        state: &gt;\n          {% set s = states('sensor.living_room_wifi_signal') %}\n          {% if not has_value('sensor.living_room_wifi_signal') %}mdi:wifi-strength-off-outline\n          {% else %}\n            {% set dbm = s | float %}\n            {% if dbm &gt;= -55 %}mdi:wifi-strength-4\n            {% elif dbm &gt;= -67 %}mdi:wifi-strength-3\n            {% elif dbm &gt;= -75 %}mdi:wifi-strength-2\n            {% elif dbm &gt;= -85 %}mdi:wifi-strength-1\n            {% else %}mdi:wifi-strength-outline\n            {% endif %}\n          {% endif %}\n</code></pre>\n<h4 id=\"weather-no-template-needed\">Weather: no template needed</h4>\n<p>A <code>weather.*</code> entity is understood directly. Its state is a condition code such as\n<code>partlycloudy</code>, so lizaIP translates it for you:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Where the variable sits</th>\n<th><code>${weather.home}</code> becomes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon, on its own: <code>${weather.home}</code></td>\n<td><code>mdi:weather-partly-cloudy</code> — the condition's icon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon, after <code>mdi:</code> or <code>mdi:weather-</code></td>\n<td>the same icon (<code>mdi:weather-${weather.home}</code> works for every condition, <code>clear-night</code> included)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip, page title text, <code>text:</code> image</td>\n<td><code>Partly cloudy</code> — the condition as Home Assistant names it, in your language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>An attribute, e.g. <code>${weather.home.temperature}</code></td>\n<td>the value as stored (<code>18</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>exceptional</code> is drawn as <code>mdi:weather-cloudy-alert</code>. A condition lizaIP doesn't know\nis shown as-is.</p>\n<h4 id=\"template-weather-icon-with-night-variants\">Template: weather icon with night variants</h4>\n<p>Only if you want more than the built-in mapping — e.g. a moon instead of a sun after\nsunset, or your own icon choices — use a template helper. It looks the condition up\nand swaps in the night variants once the sun has set. Replace <code>weather.home</code> with your\nown weather entity:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-jinja\">{% set icons = {\n  'clear-night': 'weather-night',\n  'cloudy': 'weather-cloudy',\n  'exceptional': 'weather-cloudy-alert',\n  'fog': 'weather-fog',\n  'hail': 'weather-hail',\n  'lightning': 'weather-lightning',\n  'lightning-rainy': 'weather-lightning-rainy',\n  'partlycloudy': 'weather-partly-cloudy',\n  'pouring': 'weather-pouring',\n  'rainy': 'weather-rainy',\n  'snowy': 'weather-snowy',\n  'snowy-rainy': 'weather-snowy-rainy',\n  'sunny': 'weather-sunny',\n  'windy': 'weather-windy',\n  'windy-variant': 'weather-windy-variant',\n} %}\n{% set night = is_state('sun.sun', 'below_horizon') %}\n{% set c = states('weather.home') %}\n{% if night and c == 'sunny' %}{% set c = 'clear-night' %}{% endif %}\n{% if night and c == 'partlycloudy' %}mdi:weather-night-partly-cloudy\n{% else %}mdi:{{ icons.get(c, 'weather-cloudy-alert') }}\n{% endif %}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>mdi:weather-cloudy-alert</code> stands in when the entity is unavailable or reports a\ncondition the table doesn't know. Name the helper e.g. <code>lizaIP weather icon</code>\n(entity <code>sensor.lizaip_weather_icon</code>) and use it like the others:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>You write</th>\n<th>The remote shows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Icon</td>\n<td><code>${sensor.lizaip_weather_icon}</code></td>\n<td>the current weather as an icon, a moon at night</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooltip</td>\n<td><code>${weather.home.temperature}°</code></td>\n<td>18°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Page title</td>\n<td><code>${sensor.lizaip_weather_icon}</code></td>\n<td>the weather icon as the page's title</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>As YAML in <code>configuration.yaml</code>, paste the same template under <code>state: &gt;</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">template:\n  - sensor:\n      - name: &quot;lizaIP weather icon&quot;\n        state: &gt;\n          {% set icons = {'clear-night': 'weather-night', 'cloudy': 'weather-cloudy',\n             'exceptional': 'weather-cloudy-alert', 'fog': 'weather-fog',\n             'hail': 'weather-hail', 'lightning': 'weather-lightning',\n             'lightning-rainy': 'weather-lightning-rainy',\n             'partlycloudy': 'weather-partly-cloudy', 'pouring': 'weather-pouring',\n             'rainy': 'weather-rainy', 'snowy': 'weather-snowy',\n             'snowy-rainy': 'weather-snowy-rainy', 'sunny': 'weather-sunny',\n             'windy': 'weather-windy', 'windy-variant': 'weather-windy-variant'} %}\n          {% set night = is_state('sun.sun', 'below_horizon') %}\n          {% set c = states('weather.home') %}\n          {% if night and c == 'sunny' %}{% set c = 'clear-night' %}{% endif %}\n          {% if night and c == 'partlycloudy' %}mdi:weather-night-partly-cloudy\n          {% else %}mdi:{{ icons.get(c, 'weather-cloudy-alert') }}\n          {% endif %}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The same pattern — a template helper that maps a raw state to the nearest valid icon\nname — works for anything with stepped icons:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source value</th>\n<th>Stepped icon names</th>\n<th>Helper maps …</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Battery %</td>\n<td><code>mdi:battery-10</code> … <code>-90</code>, <code>mdi:battery</code>, <code>mdi:battery-outline</code></td>\n<td>percent → nearest ten</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wi-Fi signal</td>\n<td><code>mdi:wifi-strength-outline</code>, <code>-1</code> … <code>-4</code>, <code>mdi:wifi-strength-off-outline</code></td>\n<td>dBm or % → 0–4 bars (<a href=\"#template-wi-fi-signal-icon\">template</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weather state</td>\n<td><code>mdi:weather-sunny</code>, <code>mdi:weather-rainy</code>, <code>mdi:weather-partly-cloudy</code>, …</td>\n<td>built in: <code>${weather.home}</code> alone is its icon (<a href=\"#weather-no-template-needed\">details</a>); a helper only for night variants (<a href=\"#template-weather-icon-with-night-variants\">template</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This same technique — more detail, plus the full battery walkthrough — is also in\n<a href=\"https://github.com/liza-home/home-assistant/blob/main/README.md#an-icon-that-follows-a-value--the-battery-level\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><em>An icon that follows a value — the battery level</em></a>\nin the README.</p>";
